3 PERMISSIONS
*************

You can restrict who can access hledger-web by

   * setting the IP address it listens on (see '--host' above).  By
     default it listens on 127.0.0.1, accessible to users on the local
     machine only.
   * or by putting it behind an authenticating proxy, such as caddy or
     apache
   * or by putting it behind a firewall.

   And you can restrict what the users reaching it can do, by specifying
the '--allow=ACCESSLEVEL' option at startup.  ACCESSLEVEL is one of:

   * 'view' - allows viewing the journal file(s)
   * 'add' - also allows adding new transactions to the main journal
     file
   * 'edit' - also allows editing, uploading or downloading the journal
     file(s)
   * 'sandstorm' - (for the hledger-web Sandstorm app:) allows whichever
     of 'view', 'add', or 'edit' are specified in the
     'X-Sandstorm-Permissions' HTTP header

   The default access level is 'add' when listening on a local-only
address ('127.0.0.1', '::1', 'localhost', or a unix socket), and 'view'
otherwise.  To allow more than the default access, start it with an
explicit '--allow=add' or '--allow=edit' option.


File: hledger-web.info,  Node: EDITING UPLOADING DOWNLOADING,  Next: REPORTS,  Prev: PERMISSIONS,  Up: Top

4 EDITING, UPLOADING, DOWNLOADING
*********************************

If you start hledger-web with '--allow=edit', you'll see a new "spanner"
button to the right of the search form.  Clicking this will let you
edit, upload, or download the journal file or any files it includes.

   Note, unlike any other hledger command, in this mode you (or any
visitor) can alter or wipe the data files.  Before saving an edited or
uploaded file, hledger-web saves a numbered backup of it (eg
'main.journal.1', then 'main.journal.2'); if the backup can't be
written, the file is not changed.  (Adding a transaction appends it to
the main journal file without making a backup.)  hledger-web is not
aware of version control systems, currently; if you use one, you'll have
to arrange to commit the changes yourself.

   Changes which would leave the journal file(s) unparseable or invalid
(eg with failing balance assertions) are prevented.


File: hledger-web.info,  Node: REPORTS,  Next: URL PARAMETERS,  Prev: EDITING UPLOADING DOWNLOADING,  Up: Top

5 REPORTS
*********

Besides the journal and the account registers, hledger-web shows these
reports.  The sidebar and the links at the top of every page lead to
each of them, and so does the 'g' key followed by the first letter of
the report's name:

   * *Balance sheet* ('b', '/balancesheet'): assets, liabilities and net
     worth, like the balancesheet command.  Its Equity option adds an
     equity section, like balancesheetequity.
   * *Income statement* ('i', '/incomestatement'): revenues, expenses
     and net income, like incomestatement.
   * *Cashflow* ('c', '/cashflow'): changes in cash accounts, like
     cashflow.
   * *Holdings* ('h', '/holdings'): investments and their performance,
     like holdings.  Shown only for journals that use lots; see below.
   * *All accounts* ('a', '/balance'): all accounts, or those you search
     for, like balance.

   Each report shows a chart and then a table (by default; both can be
toggled).  Click any amount to see the transactions it is made of.

